{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>And we sat in the valley, etc.</b> One needs to know the intent of the verse — what it comes to inform us in this statement, and what connection it has neither to what precedes it nor to what follows it. And our Sages of blessed memory in Sifrei interpreted the verse, and Rashi of blessed memory cited them, but according to their words of blessed memory the intent of saying <b>and we sat</b> is still not settled, since it comes only to inform us of the matter of Peor. Moreover, the phrase <b>opposite Beit Peor</b> has no meaning if the verse comes only to mention the worship of Peor — it should have said ״וַתִּצָּמֶד לְבַעַל פְּעוֹר״ (Numbers 25:3)." "And it appears that the intent of the verse is to conclude the matter it began at the beginning of the parashah: ״וָאֶתְחַנַּן אֶל ה׳״ and so on, and it finishes saying that his prayer did not help to cross the Jordan, and ״וְנֵשֶׁב בַּגָּיְא״ — יְשִׁיבָה (sitting) means nothing but permanent delay. And its saying <b>in the valley</b> is because the land of Israel is higher than all lands, as it is written (Numbers 13:17) ״עֲלוּ זֶה בַּנֶּגֶב״. And its saying <b>Beit Peor</b> alludes to one of the reasons for his burial there (Pirkei de-Rabbi Eliezer, chapter 46) — so that he would be opposite the sin of Peor, to prevent it from raising its head to prosecute against Israel:"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:14:59.284731136Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}